### What is the HashMapStateBackend?

The HashMapStateBackend holds state as live Java objects in a hash map on the task manager heap. Reads and writes are extremely fast with no serialization, giving the lowest per-access latency.

```java
import org.apache.flink.runtime.state.hashmap.HashMapStateBackend;

StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
    StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

// Configure HashMap state backend
env.setStateBackend(new HashMapStateBackend());
env.enableCheckpointing(10_000);  // checkpoint every 10 seconds

// State lives on JVM heap as plain Java objects
// Access: O(1) hash lookup, no serialization
```

```text
HashMapStateBackend:
  Storage:      JVM heap (Java objects)
  Access speed: Fastest (no serialization)
  Max state:    Limited by heap size (typically 1-50 GB)
  GC pressure:  High for large state
  Checkpoint:   Full snapshot (all state serialized and uploaded)
```

### What is the EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend?

The EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend stores state as serialized bytes in an embedded RocksDB key-value store on local disk. Every access serializes the key, looks it up in RocksDB, and deserializes the value.

```java
import org.apache.flink.contrib.streaming.state.EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend;

// Configure RocksDB state backend
env.setStateBackend(new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true));
// true = enable incremental checkpoints
env.enableCheckpointing(10_000);

// State lives on local SSD via RocksDB
// Access: serialize key → RocksDB lookup → deserialize value
```

```text
EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend:
  Storage:      Local disk (serialized bytes in RocksDB)
  Access speed: Slower (serialization + disk I/O)
  Max state:    Hundreds of GB to TB (spills to disk)
  GC pressure:  Low (state is off-heap)
  Checkpoint:   Incremental (only changed files uploaded)
```

### How are they different?

| Aspect | HashMapStateBackend | EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend |
|---|---|---|
| Storage | JVM heap | Local disk (RocksDB) |
| Access latency | Lowest (no serialization) | Higher (serialize/deserialize) |
| State size limit | Bounded by heap | Bounded by disk |
| GC pressure | High for large state | Low (off-heap) |
| Checkpoint type | Full snapshot | Incremental (only delta) |
| Best for | Small state, low latency | Large state, long-running jobs |

```java
// Small state job: HashMap
env.setStateBackend(new HashMapStateBackend());
// e.g., 1000 admin accounts, microsecond access

// Large state job: RocksDB
env.setStateBackend(new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true));
// e.g., 100 million retail accounts, incremental checkpoints
```

### Why do incremental checkpoints matter?

With HashMap, every checkpoint serializes and uploads all state. With RocksDB, Flink uploads only the RocksDB files that changed since the last checkpoint, so checkpoint cost scales with the delta. This is often the deciding factor for large-state jobs.

```java
// HashMap: full snapshot every checkpoint
// State = 50 GB → every checkpoint uploads 50 GB
// Checkpoint time: minutes

// RocksDB incremental: only delta
// State = 50 GB, changed since last = 500 MB
// Checkpoint uploads only 500 MB
// Checkpoint time: seconds
```

### How do you configure RocksDB for performance?

RocksDB benefits from fast local SSDs and from tuning its block cache and write buffers. You must also budget off-heap native memory alongside the JVM.

```java
import org.apache.flink.contrib.streaming.state.EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend;
import org.apache.flink.contrib.streaming.state.PredefinedOptions;

EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend rocksDB =
    new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true);  // incremental
rocksDB.setPredefinedOptions(
    PredefinedOptions.SPINNING_DISK_OPTIMIZED_HIGH_MEM);
env.setStateBackend(rocksDB);

// Flink config for memory:
// taskmanager.memory.managed.fraction: 0.4  (for RocksDB cache)
// state.backend.rocksdb.memory.managed: true
```

### When do you use each?

The choice comes down to two questions: how big is your state, and how tight is your latency budget.

```java
// Use case 1: Failed login tracker (small state, low latency)
// ~1000 admin accounts, need microsecond state access
env.setStateBackend(new HashMapStateBackend());

// Use case 2: Retail fraud scoring (huge state)
// 100 million accounts, 90-day rolling history per account
// State = hundreds of GB, must checkpoint efficiently
env.setStateBackend(new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true));
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using HashMapStateBackend for a large-state job, which leads to heap exhaustion and long GC pauses. Another is forgetting to budget native memory for RocksDB.

```java
// WRONG -- HashMap for 100M accounts
env.setStateBackend(new HashMapStateBackend());
// State exceeds heap → OutOfMemoryError
// Full checkpoint of 50 GB → timeout

// CORRECT -- RocksDB for large state
env.setStateBackend(new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true));
// State spills to disk, incremental checkpoints stay fast
```

### What about state TTL?

Both backends support state time-to-live (TTL) to automatically expire old state entries, which is useful for bounding state in non-windowed keyed operations.

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.StateTtlConfig;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.time.Time;

StateTtlConfig ttlConfig = StateTtlConfig
    .newBuilder(Time.days(90))  // expire after 90 days
    .setUpdateType(StateTtlConfig.UpdateType.OnCreateAndWrite)
    .setStateVisibility(
        StateTtlConfig.StateVisibility.NeverReturnExpired)
    .build();

ValueStateDescriptor<BigDecimal> desc =
    new ValueStateDescriptor<>("balance", BigDecimal.class);
desc.enableTimeToLive(ttlConfig);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Most large production Flink jobs at a bank run on RocksDB because per-account state across hundreds of millions of accounts is huge and must be durable. The per-access overhead of RocksDB is often negligible relative to network and sink latency. The decisive factor is usually incremental checkpointing, which keeps many-gigabyte state checkpointable in seconds instead of minutes.
